- op: discuss  # 6aba
  at: '2026-04-09T00:00:07Z'  # 6aba
  by: agent  # 6aba
  actor: jgstern_agent  # 6aba
  clock: 9  # 6aba
  nonce: 6aba  # 6aba
  message: "[Implications of 'skip continuation lines in the post-processor at store.py:298-303' — careful analysis as requested]\n\nWHAT 'CONTINUATION LINE' ACTUALLY MEANS HERE: ruamel.yaml folds long double-quoted scalars at width=4096 by inserting newline + indent at the fold boundary. On read-back, that whitespace collapses back to a single space. So 'continuation line' = any physical line whose bytes are the latter half of a single logical scalar.\n\nWHY PER-LINE NONCES EXIST (re-derived from ADR-0013 + the code): .ops files are append-only logs merged across concurrent agent branches. Git's 3-way merge is line-based and CAN dedupe identical lines if the merge base has them — the per-line nonce makes every line globally unique so 3-way merge has zero risk of collapsing or interleaving lines from different entries. The nonce is load-bearing for that guarantee.\n\nWHAT BREAKS IF CONTINUATION LINES LACK NONCES:\n1. Two concurrent .ops appends each containing long folded scalars whose continuation lines happen to match at the same indent — git merge could mis-align them. Probability scales with (long-scalar entries) x (fold-boundary collision rate) x (concurrent edits). For typical workloads small but nonzero.\n2. More dangerous: if a continuation line by chance matches an existing line in the merge base (e.g. an empty list '[]' inside the indented continuation), the merge base could falsely dedupe it.\n3. The structural risk is real but small. The bigger issue is the next item.\n\nTHE HARD PART: identifying continuation lines from a string scan requires partial YAML parser state — track open double-quote scalars (with backslash escapes), literal block scalars '|' (indent-terminated), folded block scalars '>' (indent-terminated), and flow-style continuations '[' / '{'. Doing this correctly is fragile. Failure modes: false-negative leaves a continuation line unprotected (silent corruption); false-positive drops a nonce from a line that should have one (silent corruption, just a different shape).\n\nSIMPLER ALTERNATIVES THAT AVOID THE PARSING PROBLEM:\n(i) Bump width to a very large value (e.g. 100_000) — folds vanish for all realistic scalars. Per-line nonces remain valid because each line still IS one whole scalar. Reviewer note in this thread already flagged the diff aesthetics; for .ops files (append-only, rarely human-edited) that may be acceptable.\n(ii) Force long strings into LITERAL block scalars (|) via a custom ruamel representer. Literal blocks preserve newlines and use a clean indent-based shape; continuation detection collapses to 'inside an open block-literal' which is much simpler than full quote-state tracking. Diffs are row-aligned (better for review than (i)).\n(iii) The combination: width=8192 as defense in depth + (ii) for any field over a small threshold (say 256 bytes) + post-processor learns about block literals via indent tracking (one boolean of state, not a parser).\n\nRECOMMENDATION: NOT option (1) (skip continuation lines via inline state tracking) — too fragile. Prefer (ii) + small width bump. The only YAML construct the post-processor then needs to be aware of is literal block scalars, identifiable by the '|' line and the increased indent of the inner lines. That is a much smaller, more auditable change than tracking quote/escape state across the whole stream.\n\nOpen question for human: are .ops files ever processed by tools other than this serializer + CSafeLoader? If a third-party YAML tool reads them (e.g. for migration or audit), the change from double-quote to block-literal style is observable and the migration plan needs to handle existing .ops files. If only this codebase reads them, no migration is required."  # 6aba
- op: discuss  # 1ead
  at: '2026-04-09T00:34:28Z'  # 1ead
  by: human  # 1ead
  actor: jgstern  # 1ead
  clock: 10  # 1ead
  nonce: 1ead  # 1ead
  message: "I do not believe migration would be an issue for us now but I also think maybe it is a moot point. If we are going with \"Bump width to a very large value (e.g. 100_000)\", then why not just bump it to infinity (it's already difficult to read, so who cares)? furthermore if I understand the git merging process correctly and how tracker interacts with that, maybe there isn't even an opportunity for difficulty of this kind to be an issue?"  # 1ead
- op: discuss  # 553b
  at: '2026-04-09T00:55:32Z'  # 553b
  by: agent  # 553b
  actor: jgstern_agent  # 553b
  clock: 11  # 553b
  nonce: 553b  # 553b
  message: "[Reply to 'why not infinity / is the merge concern moot'] Investigation findings:\n\n(1) IS THE MERGE CONCERN MOOT? Mostly yes for the auto-sync path, but NO in general — and that 'no' is the load-bearing case. Findings:\n\n(a) Auto-sync (_maybe_auto_sync → do_sync in sync.py:823) does NOT perform real 3-way git merges. It uses git plumbing (read-tree/write-tree/commit-tree) with a temporary index to build a commit on top of origin/dev sequentially — never checks out a branch (avoids breaking the editable-install Python interpreter). The resulting PR is merged server-side via Forgejo API, trying ff → rebase → merge-commit. So the auto-sync path itself never fires merge=union locally.\n\n(b) HOWEVER: real 3-way merges of .ops files DO happen when developers integrate divergent feature branches back to main. test_git_integration.py contains four explicit merge tests (test_concurrent_updates_merge_cleanly, test_identical_op_type_merge, test_rebase_preserves_both_ops, test_duplicate_create_ops_handled) that create branches with concurrent .ops edits and run real git merge commands. They verify the CRDT-like property in ADR-0013: 'concurrent edits to the same item never produce git conflicts.'\n\n(c) .gitattributes confirms: '.agent/tracker/.ops/.*.ops linguist-generated merge=union diff=tracker' (and same for tracker-workspace). The nonce-on-every-line is what makes merge=union safe — without it, two ops with identical first lines would be deduped into one fused malformed YAML, and shared internal lines (e.g. 'set:' or 'status: done') would be silently stripped from earlier ops. Both pathologies are documented in ADR-0013 and store.py:9-11.\n\n(d) Two other anti-collision mechanisms ALSO exist: per-item .ops files (different items never share a file, so cross-item collision is structurally impossible) and Lamport clock ordering (compile sorts ops by clock so order-independence holds even when merge=union interleaves). The nonce is the third leg of that tripod and addresses a different failure mode (line-level dedup) that the other two don't cover.\n\n(2) WHY NOT INFINITY? No good reason — and that's the right answer. ruamel.yaml accepts any integer for ry.width; setting it to sys.maxsize (or 2**31-1) effectively means 'never fold any scalar.' Each scalar then occupies one physical line, the per-line nonce remains structurally valid for merge=union, and the continuation-line corruption bug becomes structurally impossible. The diff aesthetics objection I raised earlier dissolves on reflection: the .ops files are ALREADY effectively unreadable (machine-generated YAML with hex nonces on every line), so 'one very long line' vs 'several long folded lines' is no worse for human review. The diff=tracker textconv driver in .gitattributes already declutters diffs by showing compiled item state instead of raw ops, so reviewers don't read the raw YAML anyway.\n\nRECOMMENDATION (revised): set ry.width = sys.maxsize (or a very large constant like 2**31-1). One-line change in store.py:289. The nonce mechanism stays exactly as-is. No literal-block-scalar representer needed. No continuation-line-skip post-processor needed. No migration of existing .ops files (the read path is unchanged — CSafeLoader reads the same wire format whether ruamel folded the dump or not). The fix is conservative, minimal, and structurally eliminates the entire bug class.\n\nTests to add stay the same as the earlier [Tests to add] entry: 5000-byte multi-byte round-trip, 5000-byte fields.<key> round-trip, 10000-byte single-line description round-trip, adversarial '  # 1234'-in-content round-trip, INV-gajap-as-of-2026-04-08 regression test. Plus one new test: an explicit guard that ry.width is set to a value larger than any plausible scalar (e.g. assert ry.width > 1_000_000) so a future refactor can't silently re-introduce folding.\n\nAwaiting your go-ahead before implementing. If you confirm, I'll move WI-pusif to in_progress and ship the fix as a normal feature PR (not a governance file change — store.py is not in the governance set)."  # 553b
